WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.868
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.660
KATHY WANG: I'm Kathy
Wang, senior director

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.550
of security at GitLab.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.830
Thank you very much
for taking the time

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:20.470
to come to listen to GitLab's
journey on Zero Trust today.

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:23.170
And this is my
colleague Phillippe,

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.690
and we're going to talk
to you about Zero Trust.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.710
So what I want to do since
this is an intermediate level

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:36.100
talk is make sure everyone
is on the same page

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.760
with what we mean by
Zero Trust, and then we

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.280
want to talk about
the specific problems

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.700
that we face at GitLab
that we were trying

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.800
to address with
Zero Trust roll out,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:53.470
which is one of our biggest
initiatives this year in 2019.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:56.380
And then, of course, I'll
talk about the roadmap

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.380
and how we came
about building this.

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:02.860
And I definitely want
you to be able to learn

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.060
from our experience so far
and take this home with you

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.850
as you build your
Zero Trust journey.

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.080
So lessons learned will
also be a part of our talk.

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.770
Traditionally, what do we do
as security practitioners?

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:28.380
We set up a perimeter-based
defense system, right.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.640
That's our defensing that most
of this security industry does.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:35.750
And we all know
this really well.

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.350
It's the hard on
the outside and soft

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:40.900
on the inside kind
of architecture

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:46.180
where if an attacker gets
access inside your environment,

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.570
they can move laterally.

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:49.090
They can persist.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:51.710
They can elevate privileges.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:53.720
They can do all of these things.

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:58.520
And we know as an industry, this
does not really work that well.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.250
But most of us employ those
type of architecture anyway.

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:10.240
So what I want to highlight
is so why Zero Trust?

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:13.240
So Google has a
flavor of Zero trust

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:18.220
that they've built backing
through our early 2010s,

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.380
and they call it BeyondCorp.

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:22.810
So if you want to
read more about that,

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:27.700
they have a ton of white papers,
great websites with content.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.420
This is their flavor
of Zero Trust.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:35.350
I'm using the term Zero Trust
because every environment

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.090
has different requirements.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:45.230
In our case and in most people's
case, what Zero Trust means

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:51.350
is that all devices that are
trying to access an end point

00:02:51.350 --> 00:02:56.150
or asset in your environment
needs to be authenticated

00:02:56.150 --> 00:03:00.250
and authorized as they do so.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.270
The user has to be
verified, validated.

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:08.690
We have to know what department
or what role they have.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.880
And then we can decide whether
they should get access to what

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.490
they're trying to access.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:18.220
All of these decisions,
by the way, are dynamic.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:19.910
They're risk-based.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:24.560
So it depends on the sensitivity
of the data on the host

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.570
that they're trying to access.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:30.980
So for example, if I
use my personal device

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:35.180
to try to access a production
level database that

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.630
has customer data on
it and customer data

00:03:38.630 --> 00:03:43.710
is high sensitivity, that
decision should be a no.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.630
You cannot do that.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:49.280
You have to use some sort
of corporate managed device

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:52.350
to access that, and you have
to be in the right role.

00:03:52.350 --> 00:03:54.920
You have to be someone
who should have access

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.180
to the production DB.

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:02.210
There's somewhat
a lot of confusion

00:04:02.210 --> 00:04:04.430
about what Zero Trust is.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:06.170
This year at RSA--

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:08.760
how many people went to RSA?

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.740
OK, so a smathering of people.

00:04:11.740 --> 00:04:14.080
They did a really
hilarious video--

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:15.760
you've got our look this up--

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.529
where they went around RSA
and they asked people, just

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:21.730
random people,
what is Zero Trust?

00:04:21.730 --> 00:04:23.860
What's your definition of it?

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:27.100
Half the people didn't
know what it was, right.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:28.900
I don't know if this
is a surprise to you

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:32.990
but it kind of is to
me, because Zero Trust

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:38.060
is one of the top three buzz
phrases at RSA this year.

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:39.610
So how does that add up?

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:40.610
I don't know.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:42.520
But this is great video.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:43.670
It's hilarious.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.460
So what I want to
do here is I want

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:50.700
to define what Zero Trust
is not just in case.

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:55.320
If you think you can buy a
single product on the market,

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.100
slap that into your environment,
and now you've got Zero Trust,

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.120
you're wrong.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.910
And if vendors tell you
that, they're wrong.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:06.990
OK, this is not a product
that you can just put in.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:08.740
It's a process.

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.160
You have to build
many components,

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.500
and those components
and how they're built

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.550
is going to vary depending
on your environment, what

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:22.740
your users are situated as,
and what your requirements are

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:26.900
and what kind of data
you're trying to protect.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:30.200
The other common
misconception about Zero Trust

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.360
is that it's new.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:34.820
That's certainly how
it came across the RSA

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.500
this year, right.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.390
The reality is
that it's not new.

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:44.670
Way back in 2005 and
maybe even before that,

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:48.600
there were working groups of
security practitioners that had

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:53.400
already met, talked about their
problems like we always do--

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:56.430
we love to talk about our
problems with security--

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:00.750
and figure it out that, hey,
we have to figure out how

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:04.230
to the de-perimeterize
our environments,

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:07.830
because those particular
crunchy on the outside and soft

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:11.750
on the inside kind of model,
who is that working for, right?

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:15.510
Pretty much no one.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.550
So it's not a new concept.

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:19.890
It's been talked about before.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:27.130
Usually companies that
implement Zero Trust do it

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:31.750
after a major breach
has already occurred.

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:34.210
That's kind of
the reality of it.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:40.000
And for cloud native companies
of which GitLab is definitely

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.730
a cloud native company,
80% of these companies

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:51.310
have not implemented Zero Trust
or even started on the process.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:56.620
20% have and usually
after a major breach.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:01.000
So I'm not-- so what I'm
saying is that at GitLab we're

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.380
trying to implement this
before a major breach.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:13.590
So what are the benefits
to having Zero Trust?

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.450
I think it's pretty
clear, so I'm not

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:17.660
going to go ahead and read
all of these out to you.

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.550
You can read it yourself.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:21.750
But fundamentally, you
just need to understand

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:25.950
that this changes the
model that traditionally we

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:32.000
deploy perimeters, and we
define basically every end

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:36.240
point in our environment
as a separate perimeter.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.650
That's the idea.

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:42.420
So what happens when an
attacker gains access

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.640
to one of your
user's credentials.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:48.940
Well, you have multiple
factor authentication

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:52.580
in place and maybe a
hardware token, for example.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.880
And that will make the
credentials a whole lot less

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.350
useful for the
attacker by themselves.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:02.380
What will happen when
attackers do get access

00:08:02.380 --> 00:08:05.410
to one of the hosts
in your environment?

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.350
Which we all know in security--

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:11.260
and this has been
evolving for the 20 years

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:14.470
that I've been doing
security myself--

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:18.910
it's not whether you will
get compromised or not.

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:21.040
Many places do get compromised.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:24.220
It's about being transparent
about what's happening,

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:27.310
making sure your customers know.

00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:29.380
And then the other
thing is about how

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:32.230
you recover from that incident.

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:34.750
That's very important.

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:38.010
So what attackers will do
is try to move laterally

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:41.100
into your environment
once they get in.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:44.740
And Zero Trust would make
that a lot more challenging.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:49.890
So what we're going to see is
many more targeted attacks,

00:08:49.890 --> 00:08:52.090
and it's going to have
to be very targeted.

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:55.570
It's going to take
a lot of resources.

00:08:55.570 --> 00:08:57.590
I'm not going to sit
up here and tell you

00:08:57.590 --> 00:09:00.420
that Zero Trust is 100% secure.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:04.920
Because then I would be lying,
because security is never 100%.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.355
But you have to understand
it's an ongoing process.

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:14.660
A couple of caveats,
I want to make sure

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.010
that everyone knows this.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:24.880
You can't build Zero Trust
in a month or a week.

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:28.480
It is a journey that probably
will take you at least nine

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.740
to 12 months to do.

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:32.650
And that's assuming
that you have

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:34.480
all of the corporate
backing that you

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.550
need to do that, right.

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:39.340
That's also part of it.

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:42.520
I will go into a lot more
detail about that later.

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:45.700
And everything that I am
going to talk about today,

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:48.430
all the components that
we considered at the lab

00:09:48.430 --> 00:09:52.690
as part of our journey it's
not the totality of everything

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:56.680
you're going to consider
for your Zero Trust journey.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:58.660
Because you will have a
different environment.

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:00.250
Your users will be different.

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:03.410
What you're trying to
protect will be different.

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:06.940
So learn from us but
don't think that this

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:08.240
is all there is to it.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:18.030
So let me go into now before
we started on the Zero Trust

00:10:18.030 --> 00:10:21.540
journey and deciding how
we're going to roadmap

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:23.370
and build this out.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:28.270
What did we have in
place already at GitLab?

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:30.220
And to give you a little
bit more background

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:32.770
about the security
team at GitLab,

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:36.350
I joined GitLab about
a year and a half ago.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:38.590
And at the time
that I joined, there

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:42.080
wasn't a formal
security team, right.

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:43.540
So think about
that for a second.

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:46.580
A year and a half ago there
wasn't a security team.

00:10:46.580 --> 00:10:50.950
So now we have a
team of almost 30.

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.830
This helps a lot, of course,
but it's been a journey

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:56.420
just to get there also.

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.810
So I don't know how big
your security teams are,

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:02.390
but that's a factor.

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:05.230
So what we had already
one of the first things

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:08.620
that I set out to build was
a data classification policy.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:10.480
And why is that important?

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:14.710
Because it's going to govern
what data is being processed

00:11:14.710 --> 00:11:18.040
or stored in your environment
and what levels of sensitivity

00:11:18.040 --> 00:11:19.520
they are, right.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:25.420
So for example for us, customer
data is of highest priority.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:28.240
That's going to be
in a red data bucket,

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.230
and we bucketize the data
types by red, orange, yellow,

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:33.250
and green.

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:36.580
So from there, we
know as we build

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:40.070
out our environment and
our Zero Trust components

00:11:40.070 --> 00:11:42.290
where to focus on first, right.

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:44.560
Focus on the red data first.

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:46.210
That's the highest sensitivity.

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:48.970
So having that policy
in place and having

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:52.150
that blessed by
everybody at the company

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:55.970
is a great step forward.

00:11:55.970 --> 00:12:01.150
We also built a Google Cloud
Security guidelines policy,

00:12:01.150 --> 00:12:04.360
and what that means is
we have an infrastructure

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:08.980
team at GitLab that builds
GitLab.com and they ensure

00:12:08.980 --> 00:12:13.780
the availability of GitLab.com,
which is our SAS product.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:19.110
And we have 2 million
plus users on GitLab.com.

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.060
How did they build
that environment?

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:23.880
Are they following
security best practices?

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:27.060
Are they using
VPCs, for example,

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:29.670
to compartmentalize
different environments.

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:35.310
Are they providing access
correctly and to best practices

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:37.080
guidelines?

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:40.410
To me a policy is
just a piece of paper

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:43.240
unless we actually enforce it.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.870
So we use tools
like Google Forseti,

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:50.520
for example, to enforce
the GCP security guidelines

00:12:50.520 --> 00:12:52.560
portion of it.

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:56.430
We also built an internal
acceptable use policy,

00:12:56.430 --> 00:13:01.370
because our users should
know what's expected behavior

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:06.260
and what's not OK behavior,
right, in our environment.

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:10.730
Basically here the rules apply,
right, in GitLab.com production

00:13:10.730 --> 00:13:11.990
environments.

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:15.050
Without that,
people do whatever,

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:18.020
and you have to rely
on good intentions.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:21.360
And that's never a good idea.

00:13:21.360 --> 00:13:24.830
We also have an HR
Database in place,

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:28.850
and I mention this because
part of Zero Trust components

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:31.220
is making sure
you know who's who

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:34.340
and what organization they're
in and what role they're in

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:37.970
and what access they should
have based on that role.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:41.300
So until you know this, you
cannot make that risk-based

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.235
decision that I was
talking about earlier.

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:49.860
One thing that's very
special to GitLab--

00:13:49.860 --> 00:13:52.170
and I don't know how
many people in here

00:13:52.170 --> 00:13:57.560
work at a 100% remote company--

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:00.210
but what that
means is people are

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:04.440
connecting from 50 plus
different countries

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.070
around the world.

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:09.540
We don't have a physical
building at GitLab.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:13.110
That's a very, very
special requirement

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:16.600
in our case that may or
may not apply to you.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:20.160
But what that means as a
security practitioner to me

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:23.490
is that I really have to
focus on credential and access

00:14:23.490 --> 00:14:25.470
management of those users.

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:27.580
I can't monitor
their home networks.

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:31.640
I can't monitor their cafes
that they're connecting from.

00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:33.660
There's no physical
office that everyone's

00:14:33.660 --> 00:14:36.540
coming into or VPNing in
through that environment

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:41.170
first before accessing anything
that doesn't work that way.

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:43.680
So we have to think
about that portion of it.

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:47.670
We don't do any self-hosting
of any services.

00:14:47.670 --> 00:14:50.250
A lot of companies run
their own email servers.

00:14:50.250 --> 00:14:51.900
We don't even do that, right.

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:53.700
We use our G Suite.

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:59.130
We use Google Gmail, and we
rely on Google Security best

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:01.410
practices to help make sure.

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:05.440
For example, Google
Enterprise has a DLP feature.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:07.590
So for sensitive
documents we might

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:10.780
be able to update DLP
information to say,

00:15:10.780 --> 00:15:14.250
hey, don't allow any
documents with these key words

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:18.250
to be able to be sent out
to the public, for example.

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:22.410
So we have to rely on
third-party SaaS providers

00:15:22.410 --> 00:15:24.280
for everything.

00:15:24.280 --> 00:15:27.210
We don't self host.

00:15:27.210 --> 00:15:29.850
We also have an
environment where everyone

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:32.430
is using Mac OS and Linux.

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:35.610
We don't have Windows
in the environment.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:39.150
You may, so your mileage
is going to vary if you do,

00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:41.580
because there's different
requirements there

00:15:41.580 --> 00:15:44.560
and different OSs to deal with.

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:47.460
So we have a fairly
homogeneous environment

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:49.998
I would call it from
a security standpoint.

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:58.030
Now that I've told you what
we had in place already,

00:15:58.030 --> 00:16:01.030
let's talk about
the first problem

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:04.112
that we're looking to solve.

00:16:04.112 --> 00:16:06.070
There's going to be a
series of three problems.

00:16:06.070 --> 00:16:07.810
I'll talk about the
first and the third,

00:16:07.810 --> 00:16:10.580
and Philippe will
talk about the second.

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:14.520
But the first problem is
managing those users, right,

00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:16.570
where, as I said,
we're connecting

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:17.900
from all over the world.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:20.440
So how exactly do
we know who they

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:24.100
are, what they are trying to
access, what type of device

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:27.760
are they using to access
that, and is it OK

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:29.290
for them to access that data?

00:16:33.290 --> 00:16:39.460
So we set out to answer
a series of questions

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:42.490
in considering this problem.

00:16:42.490 --> 00:16:45.260
We looked at endpoints.

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:48.790
We need to know not just
the endpoints meaning

00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:53.320
the laptop, the user laptops
or devices, but the endpoints

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:57.280
in our production
environment in things

00:16:57.280 --> 00:17:02.510
that we manage on our own in
Google Cloud, for example.

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:06.460
And so we looked at tools
like Uptics and Kilidem

00:17:06.460 --> 00:17:09.569
because we're a Linux,
and Mac OS shop.

00:17:09.569 --> 00:17:11.290
See this is why I
said this matters.

00:17:11.290 --> 00:17:14.290
If your windows, then you're
looking at other tools

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:15.500
potentially.

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:17.589
But we looked at
these tools, which

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:21.310
is based on OSquery, which
is very important to us

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:25.990
to gain awareness of what
those endpoints look like.

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:29.500
Building that information
into an asset database

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:30.730
is key, right.

00:17:30.730 --> 00:17:33.820
So the other piece
of it is who are

00:17:33.820 --> 00:17:35.620
these people that
are trying to connect

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.910
and should they be
able to connect.

00:17:37.910 --> 00:17:41.350
So having an org
chart and an org DB

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:43.780
is something that we
also have to build,

00:17:43.780 --> 00:17:47.080
and that's going to be based
off of our HR database.

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:54.250
We also want to-- we're
growing so fast as a company

00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:56.620
that when I joined a
year and a half ago,

00:17:56.620 --> 00:18:00.250
I was probably employee
number 200 something,

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:03.910
and now there's over 500
people a year and a half later.

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:07.840
And in another year, we're
anticipating over 1,000.

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:12.430
So think about this from a
security management standpoint.

00:18:12.430 --> 00:18:16.870
You have people on-boarding and
off-boarding every week, like,

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:19.060
a lot of people potentially.

00:18:19.060 --> 00:18:25.120
And a big security risk is to
have people leave the company

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:28.800
and then still not be updated
in your database, right.

00:18:28.800 --> 00:18:31.255
Think about how
scary that would be.

00:18:31.255 --> 00:18:33.380
That's really not ideal.

00:18:33.380 --> 00:18:37.470
So being able to
on-board, off-board,

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:40.060
get people access
to the third party

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:44.080
apps that they need access
to to do their work.

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:47.990
We looked at solutions like
Okta, Duo, Google's Cloud

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:49.720
Identity.

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:53.500
These are all solutions that
you would consider when you're

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:56.650
thinking about this problem.

00:18:56.650 --> 00:19:01.100
We also looked at that
multi-factor authentication

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:01.600
piece.

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:04.970
In our case, we considered
hardware tokens.

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:09.730
Like that makes credential that
less valuable to an attacker.

00:19:09.730 --> 00:19:11.900
So we considered UB Keys.

00:19:11.900 --> 00:19:15.970
We considered Google's
Titan Security Keys.

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:19.810
So these are all pieces that
you should also look at.

00:19:19.810 --> 00:19:23.470
And how are we
effectively enforcing

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:26.030
our data classification policy?

00:19:26.030 --> 00:19:27.610
It goes back to that, right.

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:29.920
Anything that's in
a red bucket that

00:19:29.920 --> 00:19:34.270
is considered most sensitive,
we have to protect that first.

00:19:34.270 --> 00:19:36.380
And that's our highest priority.

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:40.580
So these are the types of
questions that we ask for that.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:44.028
And now we come to problem two,
and Philippe will talk to you.

00:19:44.028 --> 00:19:46.070
PHILIPPE LAFOUCRIERE:
Thank you very much, Kathy.

00:19:46.070 --> 00:19:47.653
By the way, I'm
Philippe Lafourcriere.

00:19:47.653 --> 00:19:49.640
I'm a distinguished
engineer at GitLab.

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:51.640
I'm working in
the security team.

00:19:51.640 --> 00:19:54.020
We are working on the
security features of GitLab.

00:19:54.020 --> 00:19:57.290
So that's why we're going
to talk about securing

00:19:57.290 --> 00:19:58.590
our applications.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:00.650
So we've seen so far
how Zero Trust can

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:03.860
help us securing our
network perimeter allowing

00:20:03.860 --> 00:20:08.900
the admins to set up and
enforce policies to protect

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:13.520
this network and allow users and
devices to access our resources

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:14.240
and applications.

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:18.170
But it doesn't make any sense if
we have these policies in place

00:20:18.170 --> 00:20:20.210
and attackers can bypass
them, because we are

00:20:20.210 --> 00:20:22.820
shipping insecure applications.

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:26.840
So how do we trust what's
running on our production

00:20:26.840 --> 00:20:27.500
environments?

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:30.440
The first step is to make
sure that the applications

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:32.330
themselves are secure.

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:35.510
In the last year, we have
switched left to this--

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:38.150
we have switched security
to the left at GitLab.

00:20:38.150 --> 00:20:41.480
That was a huge leap
ahead for us especially

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:43.370
for the engineering.

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:47.930
With that, we put the security
in the middle of our process.

00:20:47.930 --> 00:20:49.910
We make sure that the
developers are involved,

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:52.580
and we make sure that
we relieve the security

00:20:52.580 --> 00:20:54.670
team from this burden.

00:20:54.670 --> 00:20:58.370
With that, we also make sure
that we educate the developers.

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:02.197
There is no excuse anymore for a
developer to say I didn't know.

00:21:02.197 --> 00:21:03.530
All the information's out there.

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:06.480
The vulnerability is right
in the middle of the Merge

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:06.980
Requests.

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:08.750
So when we want to
introduce a new change

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:11.630
in their applications and
deploy that later, it's there.

00:21:11.630 --> 00:21:14.585
We have the
remediation information

00:21:14.585 --> 00:21:16.960
direct in the Merge Request
so the developers can do that

00:21:16.960 --> 00:21:17.493
themselves.

00:21:17.493 --> 00:21:18.910
If they don't know
how to do that,

00:21:18.910 --> 00:21:22.600
they can involve the security
team but only when needed.

00:21:22.600 --> 00:21:24.730
With that, we are leaving
some space and some room

00:21:24.730 --> 00:21:27.970
for our security team to
work on the very hot topics.

00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:30.910
The ones that are
not obvious and not

00:21:30.910 --> 00:21:33.130
the longing fruits
that-- or our tools

00:21:33.130 --> 00:21:36.790
are able to catch
and to provide.

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:39.920
For every commit that we
push for our applications,

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:41.570
we are going to run
security checks.

00:21:41.570 --> 00:21:44.210
We are going to do SAST,
Dependency Scanning, Container

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:46.270
Scanning, binary to
[INAUDIBLE] that we are going

00:21:46.270 --> 00:21:50.040
to see after that, Dynamic
Application Security Testing,

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:52.780
we're during the war
package for every commit.

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:55.300
So again, the developers,
they have the information.

00:21:55.300 --> 00:21:56.710
They know exactly
what's going on

00:21:56.710 --> 00:22:00.190
and they know the
implications of their changes

00:22:00.190 --> 00:22:02.300
on the production environments.

00:22:02.300 --> 00:22:06.480
We want to be able, also, this
year to add IAST and Fuzzing.

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:08.230
If you want to take a
look at the roadmap,

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:10.063
there is a link at the
bottom of this slide.

00:22:10.063 --> 00:22:11.840
You can also reach out to us.

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:15.260
We are an open core company,
we are completely transparent.

00:22:15.260 --> 00:22:18.760
Our roadmap is public, you can
check out that directly online.

00:22:22.070 --> 00:22:23.870
So all application
is not secure.

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:26.120
How can we trust what
we are going to deploy?

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:29.040
For that, we are using
Binary Authorization,

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:32.120
which is a container
security feature that

00:22:32.120 --> 00:22:36.560
provides a policy
enforcement check point

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:39.620
to ensure only signed
and notarized images can

00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:41.430
be deployed in production.

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:43.760
With that, we want
to remove completely

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:45.950
the human from the
deployment process.

00:22:45.950 --> 00:22:49.730
So that the Pipeline would be
the one and only way to create

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:52.190
images and deploy images.

00:22:52.190 --> 00:22:56.840
The signature is going to happen
directly in the pipeline phase.

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:59.780
For that, we are relying on
the Google feature name Binary

00:22:59.780 --> 00:23:01.280
Authorization.

00:23:01.280 --> 00:23:03.350
And under the hood,
Binary Authorization

00:23:03.350 --> 00:23:05.360
is relying on
Grafeas and Kritis.

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:07.670
Grafeas is the service
to annotate the images

00:23:07.670 --> 00:23:12.950
and Kritis is the service
to enforce the policies

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:15.260
and checking the signatures.

00:23:15.260 --> 00:23:18.827
We are going to use that to
annotate the images that we

00:23:18.827 --> 00:23:19.910
will deploy to production.

00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:23.310
For example, with the results
of the security checks.

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:26.420
And if we find that some
vulnerabilities are still

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:29.550
shipping in this image, we can
block the deployment directly

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:30.650
with Kritis.

00:23:30.650 --> 00:23:33.890
So with the Google
Binary Authorization.

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:36.290
this is something that
is upcoming really soon

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:38.537
and we're dogfooding,
obviously, at GitLab.

00:23:38.537 --> 00:23:40.120
We're using GitLab
to develop Gitlabs,

00:23:40.120 --> 00:23:45.330
so we are going to use
ourselves really soon.

00:23:45.330 --> 00:23:49.400
So I told you that the Pipeline
is going to sign the image.

00:23:49.400 --> 00:23:52.310
So that means the Pipeline
has the right keys,

00:23:52.310 --> 00:23:56.030
the required keys, and only
the Pipeline as these keys.

00:23:56.030 --> 00:23:58.310
So that means we need
to be able to trust

00:23:58.310 --> 00:24:00.080
where we store these keys.

00:24:00.080 --> 00:24:06.020
For that, we are using another
Google feature name Google Key

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:08.670
Management Service.

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:12.520
This service is not available
for all the users, only

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:15.520
particular users and particular
services, like the Pipeline,

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:18.730
are able to request keys.

00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:21.970
We only have GKMS activated
and on production environment

00:24:21.970 --> 00:24:22.570
right now.

00:24:22.570 --> 00:24:26.380
But we plan we have that on
more environments in the future.

00:24:26.380 --> 00:24:29.590
So know that your
deployment chain is secure,

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:31.510
application is secure,
the deployment secure.

00:24:34.885 --> 00:24:37.810
Hackers are a bit like life,
they always find a way.

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:40.733
And they can compromise
our user accounts.

00:24:40.733 --> 00:24:42.150
Even if everything
is secure, they

00:24:42.150 --> 00:24:45.990
can always use one of our user's
account if they manage to.

00:24:45.990 --> 00:24:48.690
So how do we mitigate that?

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:50.100
We can't prevent
that, obviously,

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:55.980
but we can limit the impact
of this compromisations.

00:24:55.980 --> 00:25:00.420
For that, we are using User
and Entity Behavior Analytics.

00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:03.960
It's again, a security
feature but based this time

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:05.710
on Machine Learning
and Data Analysis.

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:08.730
We're going to analyze all
the logs, all the events, all

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:12.870
the actions on our platform
and our environments.

00:25:12.870 --> 00:25:16.560
And we will do that to look
for the [INAUDIBLE] unknowns,

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:17.970
the suspicious.

00:25:17.970 --> 00:25:19.980
So for example, if we
have developers working

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:23.670
on a weekly basis, they
work from Monday to Friday,

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:24.930
they are located in the US.

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:27.180
And then suddenly on a
Saturday morning, 3:00 AM,

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:29.110
we have a connection,
we have a git

00:25:29.110 --> 00:25:33.290
commit coming from Russia,
from China, at 3:00 AM.

00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:34.350
It's really suspicious.

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:38.520
We need to be able
to catch this,

00:25:38.520 --> 00:25:40.800
to notify the security
team, and to make sure

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:44.050
that we're going to do
further investigation,

00:25:44.050 --> 00:25:46.170
and even to block the accounts.

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:49.140
But this is all
we remediate that.

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:52.130
All of these features
are available on GitLab.

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:53.718
We are again, dogfooding.

00:25:53.718 --> 00:25:55.260
This is going to be
part of something

00:25:55.260 --> 00:25:57.470
that we call GitLab Defend.

00:25:57.470 --> 00:26:00.700
And again, you can check out
the links that are there.

00:26:00.700 --> 00:26:04.440
If you want to see what's
upcoming in the roadmap

00:26:04.440 --> 00:26:06.390
this year.

00:26:06.390 --> 00:26:07.640
With that, back to you, Kathy.

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:08.140
Thank you.

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:10.450
KATHY WANG: Thanks, Philippe.

00:26:10.450 --> 00:26:12.580
So now that Philippe
has talked to you

00:26:12.580 --> 00:26:14.710
about securing our
applications, I

00:26:14.710 --> 00:26:17.050
want to talk to you
about the third problem

00:26:17.050 --> 00:26:21.640
that we had to face here,
which is securing GitLab.com,

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:23.590
which is our infrastructure.

00:26:23.590 --> 00:26:28.690
At GitLab, this environment,
acts as both our environment

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:31.210
and customer data
environment, right?

00:26:31.210 --> 00:26:34.800
We use GitLab at work.

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:38.420
And this is part of
our dogfooding process.

00:26:38.420 --> 00:26:42.950
And it's very important to us
culturally to do that, dogfood

00:26:42.950 --> 00:26:44.815
and contribute to the product.

00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:52.650
GitLab.com is where
over two million users

00:26:52.650 --> 00:26:54.690
have GitLab accounts.

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:58.680
So we want to make sure that
there is trust built in that.

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:02.040
People trust that we're
protecting their sensitive data

00:27:02.040 --> 00:27:04.920
in the repos.

00:27:04.920 --> 00:27:08.310
So what kind of information
do we need to know

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:11.010
and what questions do
we need to ask there?

00:27:11.010 --> 00:27:14.190
One thing is making sure
that we always know,

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:17.580
have full awareness, of
vulnerability management

00:27:17.580 --> 00:27:21.760
of our end point
devices in production.

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:26.940
So we looked at solutions
like Tenable.io, in our case,

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:30.760
just making sure that that
information is always there.

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:34.610
And who owns what
in the environment?

00:27:34.610 --> 00:27:38.740
Because we dogfood all
of our own stuff, that

00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:42.730
means that engineers,
developers are setting up

00:27:42.730 --> 00:27:44.710
their own environments
for testing

00:27:44.710 --> 00:27:46.690
or for whatever purpose.

00:27:46.690 --> 00:27:50.260
And so it becomes
even more imperative

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:53.620
for us to know
what those are, who

00:27:53.620 --> 00:27:57.290
owns that, who should
have access to that.

00:27:57.290 --> 00:28:01.150
So I mentioned optics
earlier and, again, this

00:28:01.150 --> 00:28:04.630
is a very useful tool if you
haven't looked into it based

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:08.810
on osquery for that.

00:28:08.810 --> 00:28:11.780
We want to make sure
that we can mitigate

00:28:11.780 --> 00:28:15.800
DDoS attacks and other
types of abuse-type attacks

00:28:15.800 --> 00:28:17.720
on GitLab.com.

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:23.270
So to that end, we looked at
WAFs, like Fastly, Cloudflare,

00:28:23.270 --> 00:28:25.590
for example.

00:28:25.590 --> 00:28:30.320
That's something that we have
to do specific to GitLab.com.

00:28:30.320 --> 00:28:34.350
And then, if an attacker
were to gain access,

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:38.330
how do we make it more difficult
for them to move laterally?

00:28:38.330 --> 00:28:43.310
And by using VPCs, we
can compartmentalize

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:46.730
each environment in
a more clean way.

00:28:46.730 --> 00:28:50.630
And then we can use other
tools to enforce any violations

00:28:50.630 --> 00:28:53.540
to those access that's
coming from one environment

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:54.860
to another.

00:28:54.860 --> 00:28:58.250
And what we've
done is, basically,

00:28:58.250 --> 00:29:01.520
when those tools run,
like Forseti, results will

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:04.430
come into Slack, we use Slack.

00:29:04.430 --> 00:29:08.840
And, basically, do chat
ops who report violations.

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:11.990
And now the security
operations team

00:29:11.990 --> 00:29:16.990
can act on those
violations and alerts.

00:29:16.990 --> 00:29:20.260
And then, how do we
secure our environment

00:29:20.260 --> 00:29:23.720
using Google Security
best practices?

00:29:23.720 --> 00:29:26.080
This is something that
Google Security research

00:29:26.080 --> 00:29:29.440
team has thought about
for a really long time.

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:33.370
They're considered to be
leaders in this area, for sure.

00:29:33.370 --> 00:29:37.570
And we want to be able to
definitely use their knowledge

00:29:37.570 --> 00:29:41.080
about security best
practices in Google Cloud

00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:43.340
to secure our own environment.

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:47.800
So we looked at tools like
Cloud Security Command Center,

00:29:47.800 --> 00:29:49.870
that's an ongoing process.

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:53.380
We turn it on, we're
evaluating right now

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:55.030
how that works for us also.

00:29:57.890 --> 00:30:01.490
Like I said earlier, a
policy isn't that useful

00:30:01.490 --> 00:30:03.410
if you don't enforce.

00:30:03.410 --> 00:30:05.540
If you don't enforce,
you're relying

00:30:05.540 --> 00:30:10.250
on users good intentions and
we know how that goes, right?

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:13.690
People are human,
they make mistakes.

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:16.190
And that's just putting
it in a nice way.

00:30:16.190 --> 00:30:21.640
So now that I've talked
to you, and Philippe

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:24.070
has talked to you about
the top three problems

00:30:24.070 --> 00:30:27.070
that we were looking to
solve with our Zero Trust

00:30:27.070 --> 00:30:31.550
implementation, let's
talk about roadmapping.

00:30:31.550 --> 00:30:36.650
So since Zero Trust
is such a big topic

00:30:36.650 --> 00:30:40.020
and it's not something
you can build right away,

00:30:40.020 --> 00:30:43.340
it takes a long
time, it's a process.

00:30:43.340 --> 00:30:45.920
My understanding is
that Google is still

00:30:45.920 --> 00:30:50.690
building beyond corp. You know,
so it's a really big project.

00:30:50.690 --> 00:30:54.970
So how do you wrap your head
around where to get started?

00:30:54.970 --> 00:30:57.810
Well, when I started it
was like, OK, so there's

00:30:57.810 --> 00:31:02.270
so many components, how do I
exactly want to bucketize that?

00:31:02.270 --> 00:31:05.200
So what I did was I
worked with my team,

00:31:05.200 --> 00:31:09.730
and decided that we're going
to do it in three buckets.

00:31:09.730 --> 00:31:13.270
The first bucket
is anything that

00:31:13.270 --> 00:31:18.670
will touch, store,
process, customer data

00:31:18.670 --> 00:31:21.280
and is managed by us.

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:24.950
So that means our
GitLab.com infrastructure,

00:31:24.950 --> 00:31:27.850
anything in there.

00:31:27.850 --> 00:31:28.580
Things like that.

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:32.920
So that's one of our buckets
that we put components into.

00:31:32.920 --> 00:31:36.100
The other bucket is endpoints.

00:31:36.100 --> 00:31:38.500
Employee laptops,
devices that are

00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:43.210
being used to access this
environment, GitLab.com

00:31:43.210 --> 00:31:48.930
production environment, that
are individually managed.

00:31:48.930 --> 00:31:51.070
That goes into the
endpoint bucket.

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:53.670
So every component that
goes towards securing that,

00:31:53.670 --> 00:31:55.510
goes into this bucket.

00:31:55.510 --> 00:31:58.360
The third bucket,
I mentioned that we

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:03.760
don't self host anything, we
rely on third-party SaS vendors

00:32:03.760 --> 00:32:04.900
also.

00:32:04.900 --> 00:32:09.680
So anything that does not
store or process customer data,

00:32:09.680 --> 00:32:12.040
and that includes
like third-party apps

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:13.900
that we use and GitLab.

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:18.670
Like Slack, like
Zoom, like Salesforce,

00:32:18.670 --> 00:32:23.920
like other third-party
apps goes into this bucket.

00:32:23.920 --> 00:32:28.780
So that's how I began to wrap
my head around exactly how we're

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:32.480
going to compartmentalize this.

00:32:32.480 --> 00:32:35.170
So based on those
three buckets, I

00:32:35.170 --> 00:32:40.060
realized that, hey,
there's no reason why

00:32:40.060 --> 00:32:42.250
we have to create
a roadmap where

00:32:42.250 --> 00:32:44.950
it's kind of done in serial.

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:48.610
For each bucket, I'm going to
have different components that

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:50.980
apply to that bucket.

00:32:50.980 --> 00:32:54.560
It's OK to build
these out in parallel.

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:58.220
That's actually more efficient,
it saves you more time.

00:32:58.220 --> 00:33:01.010
You can get to the
journey faster.

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:05.020
And so that's how we
decided to roadmap this out.

00:33:05.020 --> 00:33:07.020
I'm not going to read the
whole roadmap for you.

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:08.780
you can see for yourself.

00:33:08.780 --> 00:33:12.140
But the point of
laying it out this way,

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:15.620
that's very important to make
sure you take home with you,

00:33:15.620 --> 00:33:18.550
is things can be built
in parallel once you

00:33:18.550 --> 00:33:20.780
bucketize how it's going to be.

00:33:24.970 --> 00:33:26.610
So now that I've
talked about how

00:33:26.610 --> 00:33:29.550
we began to put the roadmap
map together and start

00:33:29.550 --> 00:33:31.350
building these components out.

00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:34.140
Let's talk about
lessons learned so far.

00:33:34.140 --> 00:33:35.850
I mean, obviously,
we're not totally

00:33:35.850 --> 00:33:37.950
done building Zero Trust.

00:33:37.950 --> 00:33:41.130
We've started and we have
some components in place.

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:45.010
But it's going to be a while
before we're completely

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:47.370
at the point where,
I would call, done

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:48.420
and then maintaining.

00:33:53.180 --> 00:33:56.840
First thing to remember,
it's an ongoing exercise.

00:33:56.840 --> 00:33:58.640
You're always gonna
get new users,

00:33:58.640 --> 00:34:01.640
new devices, new endpoints.

00:34:01.640 --> 00:34:04.010
Every single time that
happens, that information

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:06.870
needs to be updated
in your databases,

00:34:06.870 --> 00:34:08.870
in your decision process.

00:34:08.870 --> 00:34:11.010
Who gets access to what?

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:13.159
And with what device?

00:34:13.159 --> 00:34:15.940
That's always ongoing.

00:34:15.940 --> 00:34:17.900
Build each roadmap
map independently,

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:21.610
but figure out what buckets
you're going to have first.

00:34:21.610 --> 00:34:24.219
That will be the most
efficient process to use.

00:34:27.070 --> 00:34:30.150
We figured out, while putting
the roadmap map together,

00:34:30.150 --> 00:34:32.100
that there are
certain components,

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:35.550
like centralized single sign
on, that if you get that

00:34:35.550 --> 00:34:38.969
in place first earlier
on in the roadmap, some

00:34:38.969 --> 00:34:42.840
of the other pieces that follow
will be more easily facilitated

00:34:42.840 --> 00:34:45.460
because of the presence of that.

00:34:45.460 --> 00:34:48.030
So think about things
that way, as well,

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:50.639
as you put numbers on
the different components

00:34:50.639 --> 00:34:52.560
in your roadmap.

00:34:52.560 --> 00:34:56.310
What should you do first
so that what follows

00:34:56.310 --> 00:35:00.210
is more easily put in place.

00:35:00.210 --> 00:35:04.600
There is an element of user
experience that a lot of people

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:10.210
don't realize or talk about
with building out Zero Trust.

00:35:10.210 --> 00:35:14.410
You need to make sure you don't
impede business processes.

00:35:14.410 --> 00:35:17.350
I mean, if you have
Zero Trust in place

00:35:17.350 --> 00:35:19.000
but people can't
get the work done,

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:21.160
that's going to be a big no.

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:24.910
And as a security practitioner
and a security team

00:35:24.910 --> 00:35:28.500
you'll be under a lot of
fire for that kind of thing.

00:35:28.500 --> 00:35:33.640
So think about the user
experience as you go along.

00:35:33.640 --> 00:35:37.520
In our case, we
decided to go with Okta

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:39.940
for a single sign on solution.

00:35:39.940 --> 00:35:43.360
And that really, really
helped facilitate

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:45.970
the onboarding and
offboarding process.

00:35:45.970 --> 00:35:49.285
And our sales and
our marketing teams,

00:35:49.285 --> 00:35:51.160
they were really happy
with that because they

00:35:51.160 --> 00:35:53.080
have so many third-party
apps that they

00:35:53.080 --> 00:35:56.500
use that they didn't want
to sign into each one

00:35:56.500 --> 00:36:00.460
individually, separately.

00:36:00.460 --> 00:36:04.330
It made for really, really
good user experience

00:36:04.330 --> 00:36:06.220
right off the start.

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:09.820
It helps to build user
trust, which will then

00:36:09.820 --> 00:36:12.880
help you be able to roll
out other components later

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:17.800
on, which may introduce more
friction in the day to day work

00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:19.332
process.

00:36:19.332 --> 00:36:21.040
I'm not going to sit
up here and tell you

00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:24.760
security never introduces
friction, it does.

00:36:24.760 --> 00:36:28.780
But the idea is to minimize
it, make sure people trust what

00:36:28.780 --> 00:36:32.380
you're doing, and they will if
they have a positive experience

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:33.820
at the beginning.

00:36:33.820 --> 00:36:38.980
And then you can introduce
other parts of it.

00:36:38.980 --> 00:36:43.520
But always think about that
as a security practitioner.

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:46.170
We realized that there
is serious automation

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:49.050
work that needs
to be done to make

00:36:49.050 --> 00:36:51.090
these risk-based decisions.

00:36:51.090 --> 00:36:56.100
Data has to be pulled
from org DB, asset DB, all

00:36:56.100 --> 00:36:59.610
of those components to be
able to make a decision on

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:02.860
whether this access
is allowed or not.

00:37:02.860 --> 00:37:08.495
So I do have a team of
security automation engineers

00:37:08.495 --> 00:37:10.470
on my security team.

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:13.670
And this is what they
work on, how to automate

00:37:13.670 --> 00:37:15.770
all of these pieces.

00:37:15.770 --> 00:37:19.760
And that helps us scale
and it's important to scale

00:37:19.760 --> 00:37:22.680
because we're going to grow to
over 1,000 employees this year.

00:37:22.680 --> 00:37:25.700
And I can't imagine
these decision processes

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:27.010
being manual.

00:37:27.010 --> 00:37:27.890
It shouldn't be.

00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:34.060
Your mileage is going to vary.

00:37:34.060 --> 00:37:37.540
As I mentioned earlier,
you can't buy one product,

00:37:37.540 --> 00:37:40.140
slap it in, now
you got Zero Trust.

00:37:40.140 --> 00:37:42.250
You have different
requirements, most likely.

00:37:42.250 --> 00:37:46.810
Maybe you're not 100% remote,
maybe 50% of your employees

00:37:46.810 --> 00:37:50.210
come to a physical
office every day.

00:37:50.210 --> 00:37:53.000
So one size does not fit all.

00:37:53.000 --> 00:37:55.030
And as I mentioned
earlier, who goes

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:58.180
BeyondCorp is a
flavor of Zero Trust.

00:37:58.180 --> 00:38:01.600
It's a really good
learning experience

00:38:01.600 --> 00:38:04.650
to read all of the white
papers that they have,

00:38:04.650 --> 00:38:05.950
it's really great.

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:08.860
But at the end of the
day, through Google

00:38:08.860 --> 00:38:11.200
they have a different
environment than what we had.

00:38:11.200 --> 00:38:14.500
So we couldn't do
exactly the same thing.

00:38:14.500 --> 00:38:16.030
It just doesn't work.

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:18.500
One size does not fit all.

00:38:18.500 --> 00:38:22.000
And I want to also emphasize
that for some of you

00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:26.350
who may come from more
mature environments

00:38:26.350 --> 00:38:29.210
where the company has
been around for 20 years,

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:33.360
for example, and
perimeter-based protections

00:38:33.360 --> 00:38:35.950
are already put in place.

00:38:35.950 --> 00:38:38.730
It may be more of an
uphill battle for you

00:38:38.730 --> 00:38:41.610
to lobby for Zero Trust.

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:43.300
I mean, everyone wants it.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:46.772
And everyone knows
that it's a good model.

00:38:46.772 --> 00:38:49.730
It's not perfect, nothing is.

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:54.980
But you may face more
resistance because people

00:38:54.980 --> 00:38:59.210
don't like ripping up
what's already there.

00:38:59.210 --> 00:39:02.330
That's difficult.
So that's maybe

00:39:02.330 --> 00:39:05.680
a political battle for you.

00:39:05.680 --> 00:39:09.730
In GitLab's case, because
we're growing so fast.

00:39:09.730 --> 00:39:14.110
A year and a half ago, we didn't
have a formal security team.

00:39:14.110 --> 00:39:15.340
Things are different.

00:39:15.340 --> 00:39:19.390
So I want to make sure
that you know that.

00:39:19.390 --> 00:39:22.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

